}

OnikiriItem: {
	// DisplayName: Onikiri
	/* Tooltip:
		'''
		Hold left-click to drive the five-beat Crimson Rend combo; landing hits builds Stance
		Press [DASH] to spend Vigor on a Kamui Flash Step
		The Domain Expansion key unfolds or folds the Oni Domain; the Flip Domain key turns it between Omote and Ura
		In Omote, hold [SAKURA] to cruise as sakura petals (a dash chains into it too); release or run dry to reform on the spot
		In Ura, aim at a foe and left-click to dismember its true body; the wielder then suffers a 25% max-life backlash
		In the short window after a dash or Sakura Flight ends, left-click for a Zanshin Strike
		At half Stance or more, press [DASH] again mid-dash or within a second after it ends to chain a free execution dash
		After an execution dash, left-click for Annihilating Flash; at full Stance, an execution dash that pierces its target unleashes Finale Slash directly
		At full Stance, press [EXECUTE] to auto-lock, dash, and unleash Finale Slash; with no target it strikes toward the cursor
		An execution dash costs no Vigor; one that never converts loses nothing
		[Lang4]
		legend_Text
		''' */
}

KikasaItem: {
	// DisplayName: Kikasa
	/* Tooltip:
		'''
		While held, Kikasa trails behind your shoulder and tips over to sprinkle ink at nearby foes when you aren't attacking
		Hold left click to hover the umbrella overhead, dropping homing ink rain to a steady beat
		Hold right click to charge ink upside down; release to pour out an ink torrent
		While held, press Domain Expansion to raise the Blood Lake; press again to close it
		Once the water reaches your feet, hold an item and press Sink into the Lake to store it; reclaim it in the Lakeheart
		Press the same key aiming at an enemy to drown it; a drowned boss leaves behind a sunken shade
		Click the wind chime at the bottom left to open the Lakeheart and seat shades in three slots; they fight for you like minions
		Hold the wheel key to call shades out or back one by one; the fewer afield, the harder each one hits
		The gold-flame sector on the wheel lights the ghost fire, which spreads along the shore and scorches foes it touches
		At full water, tap Flip the Lake (middle mouse by default) to flip between Blood Lake and Ghost Rain
		Foes slain under the ghost rain rise as umbrella thralls that fight for you
		In ghost rain at full water, press Self-Restart to wind everything nearby back a few seconds, refilling your life and rinsing off debuffs
		At full water, hold Flip the Lake to sink into the Ghost Dream: foes there are helpless, and no ranged projectile can exist
		Items are sealed in the dream; hold left click to loose the hounds instead, and press the same key again to wake
		Press Domain Teleport to slip through a pool of water to your cursor; swifter within the Blood Lake
		More minion slots quicken the rain's beat, add more drops, and speed up the charge
		4 slots: each beat throws one extra drop at a second target
		7 slots: every fourth beat hurls every drop at once
		10 slots: heavy drops leave burning ink puddles; a full torrent bursts into three ink geysers
		[Lang4]
		legend_Text
		"No rain under the umbrella, only the sound of water"
		''' */
}

KikasaTalismanLin: {
	// DisplayName: Fine Rain Talisman
	// Tooltip: Faster rain beats, denser still as the rain holds; each drop slightly lighter
	// Origin: The old scribe said: no need to trouble the Dragon King for rain—one Fine Rain Talisman will do. Small drops are fine, so long as they never stop
	// Power: "'Fine Rain': ink-rain tempo +20%, drops falling one after another; about ten seconds of unbroken rain builds 'Lin': a drizzle veil hangs beneath the umbrella and the tempo quickens another 10%; the buildup fades when the rain stops, and recalling the umbrella halves it at once"
	// Burden: Drop damage -6%
}

KikasaTalismanLao: {
	// DisplayName: Ink Puddle Talisman
	// Tooltip: Ink drops always pool into puddles; puddles drink the rain, rising until they overflow; direct hits slightly lighter
	// Origin: The scribe hated seeing rain dry up the moment it landed—a whole inkfall wasted—so he drew this talisman to keep the rain on the ground a while longer
	// Power: "'Pooling': ink drops always pool into puddles on landing (no need to reach the fourth tier, Lake Tilt), and puddles last +50% and spread +30%; drops merging into an existing puddle raise its water level, and the third one overflows it: the puddle widens a step and slaps out overflow surges to both sides (50% puddle damage each)"
	// Burden: Drop direct damage -5%
}

KikasaTalismanPei: {
	// DisplayName: Downpour Talisman
	// Tooltip: Faster inverted charging; a full bowl tips like a floodgate; steady rain slightly slower
	// Origin: The worst prayer for rain is a half-hearted one. This talisman answers a single request: if it rains, let it pour
	// Power: "'Downpour': inverted charge rate +35%, ink geyser damage +20%; at full charge the bowl brims over with beads and a low hum, and pouring right then opens the gate: a burst erupts at the fall's source (80% pour damage) and the inkfall runs wider"
	// Burden: Ink-rain tempo 8% slower
}

KikasaTalismanSha: {
	// DisplayName: Swift Rain Talisman
	// Tooltip: The umbrella rises near-instantly; the opening beat drops three rapid drops in a row; the beat after lags slightly
	// Origin: The scribe met robbers on a night road—umbrella up, rain down, umbrella away, all in a blink. Afterward he wrote that one word, "fast", into a talisman
	// Power: "'Swift Rain': umbrella rise time -75%; the opening beat always drops a rapid triple (75% damage each)"
	// Burden: After the triple, the next beat's interval +40%
}

KikasaTalismanYin: {
	// DisplayName: Ink Bloom Talisman
	// Tooltip: Drop hits stack ink marks; at five stacks they bloom into a burst; direct hits slightly lighter
	// Origin: Leave a drop of ink on wet paper and it blooms into a flower by itself. The scribe said enemies work the same way—land a few more drops, and they bloom
	// Power: "'Ink Bloom': ink drops leave marks on hit (up to five stacks, each bleeding over time); at five stacks the ink blooms, dealing 160% drop damage and clearing the stacks"
	// Burden: Drop direct damage -8%
}

KikasaTalismanLu: {
	// DisplayName: Sweet Dew Talisman
	// Tooltip: Drop hits sometimes condense dewdrops; picking one up restores life; rain beats slightly slower
	// Origin: The scriptures say sweet dew falls from heaven and wards off all illness. The scribe never managed that miracle, so he settled for the next best thing: a little sweetness saved up in every drop
	// Power: "'Sweet Dew': ink drops have a 12% chance on hit to condense a dewdrop; picking one up restores 2 life; at most three afield"
	// Burden: Ink-rain tempo 6% slower
}

KikasaTalismanFei: {
	// DisplayName: Lingering Mist Talisman
	// Tooltip: Every third drop becomes a mist-drop, leaving a slowing, searing fog where it lands; the mist-drop itself is lighter
	// Origin: Halfway down a mountain, rain often turns to mist that clings and will not let go. The scribe found that clinging worse than any raindrop, so he took it into the talisman and put it to work
	// Power: "'Lingering Mist': every third ink drop becomes a mist-drop—its impact leaves a fog for two seconds, and foes inside take damage over time and lose 10% speed"
	// Burden: Mist-drop damage -30%
}

KikasaTalismanXian: {
	// DisplayName: Scattering Pearls Talisman
	// Tooltip: Large drops shatter into five bouncing ink pearls on landing; puddles slightly smaller
	// Origin: Heavy rain on flagstones sends beads leaping waist-high and soaks you through. The scribe wrote that leap into the talisman, and taught the beads to bite
	// Power: "'Scattering Pearls': large drops shatter on landing into five ink pearls (30% damage each), and each pearl can bounce twice more"
	// Burden: Puddle radius -15%
}

KikasaTalismanXi: {
	// DisplayName: Tide Talisman
	// Tooltip: Puddles breathe like the tide and surge at nearby foes; puddle lifespan slightly shorter
	// Origin: The scribe lived three years by the sea and learned exactly one thing: water does not wait to be stepped in—it goes over by itself
	// Power: "'Tide': ink puddles gain the tide's nature—they swell and ebb, and fresh ink landing in one sends it surging at the nearest foe, the wave-edge dealing 50% puddle damage"
	// Burden: Puddle lifespan -20%
}

KikasaTalismanZi: {
	// DisplayName: Ink Soak Talisman
	// Tooltip: Foes soaking in puddles gain stacks that deepen the ink damage they take; the puddle itself is lighter
	// Origin: Cloth must steep in the vat three full days before the dye takes. The scribe once dyed cloth, and knows better than anyone what "soaking" can do
	// Power: "'Ink Soak': foes standing in an ink puddle keep gaining soak (up to eight stacks); each stack increases the ink damage they take by 2.5%"
	// Burden: Puddle damage -25%
}

KikasaTalismanTing: {
	// DisplayName: Thunder Geyser Talisman
	// Tooltip: Ink geysers become thunder columns that chain lightning to nearby foes; cascades sometimes raise a small thunder-geyser on landing; charging slightly slower
	// Origin: Nothing is more frightening than a lake in a thunderstorm—lightning strikes the water and the whole lake lights up with it. The scribe drew this talisman from that one sight
	// Power: "'Thunder Geyser': geyser columns +50% taller, and hits chain lightning to up to two nearby foes (50% geyser damage each); even an unfilled cascade has a 25% chance to raise a small thunder-geyser on landing"
	// Burden: Inverted charge rate -13%
}

KikasaTalismanHong: {
	// DisplayName: Rainbow Bridge Talisman
	// Tooltip: After a cascade ends, a rainbow bridge arches up—buffs beneath it, burns along its span; the cascade is slightly narrower
	// Origin: A rainbow after rain is a good omen. The scribe simply drew the omen into the talisman: the moment the rain stops, the bridge goes up, and everyone beneath it shares the luck
	// Power: "'Rainbow Bridge': when a cascade ends, a rainbow arches over the landing point for five seconds—+10% movement speed and +10% summon damage beneath it, and foes crossing the span take 40% cascade damage every half second"
	// Burden: Cascade width -10%
}

KikasaTalismanHang: {
	// DisplayName: Night Miasma Talisman
	// Tooltip: Ink damage rises at night and puddles steam up miasma columns; slightly weaker by day
	// Origin: The old saying goes—never touch the night vapors. The scribe bottled some anyway, and the saying proved true. So now it is the enemy's problem
	// Power: "'Night Miasma': ink damage +15% at night, and ink puddles keep steaming up miasma columns (30% drop damage every half second)"
	// Burden: Ink damage -5% by day
}

KikasaTalismanBao: {
	// DisplayName: Hailstone Talisman
	// Tooltip: Every fourth beat all drops become great hail—armor-breaking heavy hits that leave no puddles; ordinary beats slightly lighter
	// Origin: Hail is the rain's violent temper—it smashes tiles and basins and no one can stop it. The scribe never tried to tame it; he only struck a deal: every four beats, one beat is yours
	// Power: "'Hailstone': every fourth beat is a guaranteed full volley, all drops turned to great hail—size x1.6, damage x2.2, hits break armor and knock back hard; great hail leaves no puddles"
	// Burden: Ordinary-beat drop damage -8%
}

KikasaTalismanShu: {
	// DisplayName: Timely Rain Talisman
	// Tooltip: For three seconds after you are hurt, the rain doubles its pace and every drop heals; ordinary tempo slightly slower
	// Origin: In the old tales, the one who arrives just when help is needed is called "Timely Rain". This talisman follows the same rule: the moment the umbrella's master takes a blow, the rain must arrive at once
	// Power: "'Timely Rain': for three seconds after you are hurt, rain-beat interval is halved, the rain turns gold, and every drop that hits restores 1 life; twelve-second cooldown"
	// Burden: Ordinary rain-beat interval +5%
}

KikasaTalismanLong: {
	// DisplayName: Scouring Falls Talisman
	// Tooltip: The cascade becomes a torrent that shoves foes along the flow; the cascade runs shorter
	// Origin: When a flash flood comes down, no one keeps their footing. The scribe watched one from a clifftop, came home, and wrote a single word into the talisman: "rush"
	// Power: "'Scouring Falls': the cascade becomes a torrent—its body keeps shoving foes on the fall line along the flow, and direct hits from the cascade knock back +35%"
	// Burden: Cascade duration -20%
}

KikasaTalismanShuang: {
	// DisplayName: Frost Mirror Talisman
	// Tooltip: Puddles freeze into frost mirrors—treading slows foes, drop hits shatter the mirror for a burst; puddles cannot be renewed
	// Origin: After Frost's Descent, puddle water freezes overnight into thin ice—it looks like a mirror, until you step on it. What the scribe collected was exactly that layer of "looks harmless"
	// Power: "'Frost Mirror': large drops freeze into frost puddles where they land (no need to reach the fourth tier, Lake Tilt); the mirror no longer sears—treading foes are slowed 40%, and an ink drop striking the mirror shatters it at once, bursting for damage scaled to its remaining life and raising ice spikes"
	// Burden: Each mirror stands alone—puddles cannot merge to renew their life
}

KikasaTalismanZha: {
	// DisplayName: Rain Drum Talisman
	// Tooltip: Land hits on seven straight beats and the eighth strikes at triple damage; the rain rests one beat after
	// Origin: Raindrops on an umbrella are already a drum. The scribe heard the trick of it: miss not a single beat, and the eighth rings loudest
	// Power: "'Rain Drum': a seven-point drum-ring hangs at the umbrella's crown—land at least one drop on each of seven straight beats, and the eighth beat strikes with all drops at x3 damage, like a massed drum; miss a beat and the count restarts"
	// Burden: After the heavy beat the rain rests one beat
}

KikasaTalismanNi: {
	// DisplayName: Tricolor Talisman
	// Tooltip: Drops cycle three dyes—red bursts, cyan slows, violet deepens wounds; drop damage slightly reduced
	// Origin: "'Ink holds five colors,' goes the saying in the trade. The scribe could only afford three—but red bursts, cyan clings, violet corrodes, and that is plenty"
	// Power: "'Tricolor': ink drops take three dyes in turn—red drops burst on hit (40% splash), cyan drops slow foes 15%, violet drops increase ink damage taken by 5%"
	// Burden: Drop damage -5%
}

KikasaTalismanWen: {
	// DisplayName: Cloud Script Talisman
	// Tooltip: Three script-stars circle the umbrella and charge on hits; at full charge one hurls itself as a homing ink bolt; rain beats slightly slower
	// Origin: Cloud script is the writing the Dao sets on clouds; no mortal hand can copy it. The scribe copied what he could—the characters came out wrong, but they learned to fly
	// Power: "'Cloud Script': three cloud-script stars circle the umbrella's rim, charged by every drop hit; when the charge fills, the star on duty hurls itself as a homing ink bolt (120% drop damage)"
	// Burden: Rain-beat interval +8%
}

KikasaTalismanPo: {
	// DisplayName: Moonsoul Talisman
	// Tooltip: Night hits wax an ink moon; at full moon the next cascade becomes a moonfall; useless by day
	// Origin: Practicing at night, the scribe noticed a small moon over the umbrella: the more he struck, the rounder it grew. Feed it to the fifteenth, and it pours one grand fall
	// Power: "'Moonsoul': at night an ink moon hangs over the umbrella, waxing with every hit; at full moon the next cascade becomes a moonfall—width x1.5, its strikes leaving moon-mark bursts (35% splash)"
	// Burden: This talisman does nothing by day
}

KikasaTalismanXiao: {
	// DisplayName: Nine Heavens Talisman
	// Tooltip: The umbrella climbs high; drops plunge faster and harder; rain beats slightly slower
	// Origin: The same raindrop makes a splash from the eaves, and a crater from the ninth heaven. The scribe's reasoning was exactly that simple
	// Power: "'Nine Heavens': umbrella hover height doubled, drops turned to high plunges—terminal speed +40%, damage +25%, and a rain-shadow ring surfaces at the landing point first"
	// Burden: Rain-beat interval +10%
}

KikasaTalismanMei: {
	// DisplayName: Mold Rain Talisman
	// Tooltip: Drop hits stack mold-rot; molded foes burst into infectious spores on death; direct hits slightly lighter
	// Origin: Forty days of plum rain, and mold grew in the talisman chest. The scribe could not bring himself to wipe it off—the rain raised it too, so it counts as family
	// Power: "'Mold Rain': ink drops apply Mold-Rot on hit (up to ten stacks, bleeding per stack); when a molded foe dies it bursts into a spore cloud, passing half its stacks to nearby foes with a small hit"
	// Burden: Drop direct damage -10%
}

KikasaTalismanPi: {
	// DisplayName: Heavenly Thunder Talisman
	// Tooltip: For ten seconds after a cascade is poured, drop hits sometimes call a thunderbolt straight down; geysers slightly lighter
	// Origin: The Five Thunders method of the Dao teaches "draw the lightning with the rain". The scribe never mastered the first half—luckily, rain he has in abundance
	// Power: "'Heavenly Thunder': each finished cascade opens a ten-second window—within it, drop hits have a 20% chance to call a thunderbolt straight down on the point struck (180% damage along the whole bolt's path)"
	// Burden: Geyser damage -20%
}

KikasaTalismanXia: {
	// DisplayName: Twilight Blaze Talisman
	// Tooltip: At dawn and dusk ink damage burns fiercer and drop hits ignite glow-flame; slightly weaker at noon and midnight
	// Origin: The glow is rain set alight at the sky's edge. The scribe gathered a handful before sunrise—the paper baked yellow, but the talisman took
	// Power: "'Twilight Blaze': for a stretch around sunrise and sunset, ink damage +18% and drop hits ignite a brief Glow-Flame burn; in those hours the whole rain-kit turns orange-gold"
	// Burden: Ink damage -6% at noon and midnight
}

KikasaTalismanJi: {
	// DisplayName: Clear Skies Talisman
	// Tooltip: Hits while the umbrella is up store clear-sky force; on recall a beam of daylight crashes down at the cursor; damage slightly reduced while the umbrella is up
	// Origin: The moment rain gives way to clear sky, a pillar of light slips through the cloud seam. The scribe kept watch for half a month and took its rubbing before the clouds could close
	// Power: "'Clearing': hits while the umbrella is raised keep storing clear-sky force; the instant it is recalled the clouds part, and a beam of daylight crashes down at the cursor—damage scales with what was stored, fiercest at full"
	// Burden: Ink damage -8% while the umbrella is raised
}

KikasaTalismanYu: {
	// DisplayName: Rain Dance Talisman
	// Tooltip: Hits build the rite; at full it opens the altar—denser rain, fiercer drops, puddles and geysers fully unlocked, three geysers at the close; slightly duller outside the rite
	// Origin: To pray for rain, the ancients raised an altar, danced upon it, and sounded all eight instruments—a rite named the Rain Dance. The scribe folded the whole ceremony into one talisman: the umbrella is the shaman, and the rain is the answer
	// Power: "'Great Rite': ink-rain hits build the rite; at full it opens the altar of itself for ten seconds—rain beats halved, drops dyed rainbow at +15% damage and all of them pooling, geysers fully unlocked; when the rite closes, three ink geysers erupt beneath the umbrella"
	// Burden: Ink damage -10% outside the Great Rite
}

SHPCItem: {
	DisplayName: SHPC
	Tooltip: ""
}

AvalancheM60: {
	DisplayName: Lawine M60
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Verwendet Schneebälle als Munition
		Feuert eine große Menge Schneebälle ab; die Feuerkraft steigt mit abnehmender Munition
		Alle 20 Schüsse löst eine Überlastexplosion aus, die eine kleine Lawine erzeugt
		"Denji, lass uns eine Schneeballschlacht machen"
		'''
}

CrystalDimming: {
	DisplayName: Eiszeit
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Verwendet Schneebälle als Munition
		Feuert Kristallgeschosse ab, die am Einschlagpunkt Gletschersäulen aus dem Boden emporsteigen lassen
		Säulen wachsen höher, wenn sie in der Nähe bestehender Kristallcluster auftauchen
		Rechtsklick entfesselt eine Gletscherwelle, die entlang des Geländes einen stetig wachsenden Eisgrat auftürmt
		"Die Erde erinnert sich an jeden Frost"
		'''
}

DarkFrostSolstice: {
	DisplayName: Dunkelfrostflut
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Verwendet Schneebälle als Munition
		Feuert Hagel und Polarflammen ab
		Angriffe können Gegner einfrieren
		Alle 20 Schüsse löst eine Überlastexplosion aus und erzeugt am Cursor eine gewaltige Eissäule
		'''
}

SnowQuay: {
	DisplayName: Schneequay M16
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Verwendet Schneebälle als Munition
		Linksklick gedrückt halten, um einen dichten Schneestrom auf Feinde zu blasen
		Beim Blasen sammelt sich Druck in der Verdichtungskammer
		Linksklick loslassen mit genügend Druck, um eine verdichtete Schneekugel abzufeuern
		"Der Winterspaß beginnt mit einem Schneepustgerät"
		'''
}

NeutronGlaive: {
	DisplayName: Neutronengleve
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Schwinge einen starken Gravitationswellenschwertstoß, der bei Treffer eine Neutronensternexplosion erzeugt
		Halte die rechte Maustaste gedrückt, um das Schwert aufzuladen; bei voller Ladung und Loslassen entsteht am Cursor eine große Neutronensternexplosion, sonst wird die gespeicherte Energie freigesetzt
		'''
}

NeutronBow: {
	DisplayName: Neutronenbogen
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Feuere Gravitationspfeile ab, die bei Treffer Gammastrahlen erzeugen
		Halte die rechte Maustaste für Dreistufenladung gedrückt. Wenn die Energie vollständig aufgeladen ist, werden drei starke Gravitationspfeile abgefeuert
		'''
	Lang1: Fängt Schwerkraft ein
	Lang2: Zwingt Schwerkraft in die Knie
	Lang3: Bereit!!
}

NeutronGun: {
	DisplayName: Sternenjäger
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Feuere Gravitationsgeschosse ab, die bei Treffer einen Verzerrungspunkt erzeugen
		Drücke die rechte Maustaste, um in den Scharfschützenmodus zu wechseln und stärkere Gravitationsgeschosse abzufeuern. Die Geschosse erzeugen bei Treffer Gammastrahlen, die herabregnen
		Jeder Schuss lädt die Waffe auf. Wenn die Ladung vollständig ist und erneut geschossen wird, wird an der Mausposition eine Neutronensternexplosion ausgelöst
		"Schieße die Sonne herunter!"
		'''
}

NeutronWand: {
	DisplayName: Sternengraberlicht
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Feuere vier starke Gravitationssterne ab
		Halte die rechte Maustaste gedrückt, um an der Mausposition Gravitationsmagie auszuführen
		"Es wird erzählt, dass ein mächtiger psychischer Begabter in der Antike diesen Stab benutzte, um Sterne zu einem Schwarzen Loch zu komprimieren, aber es gibt keine Bestätigung, nur ein Fragment von einem alten Gedicht, das von den interstellaren Nomaden gesungen wird"
		'''
}

NeutronScythe: {
	DisplayName: Schwarzloch-Bote
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Eine mit hoher Geschwindigkeit rotierende Neutronensense, die Gammastrahlen auf nahe Feinde abfeuert und entlang ihrer Flugbahn Verzerrungs-Detonationspunkte hinterlässt
		Rechtsklick: Entfesselt einen Schwarzlochausbruch, der dreizehn Sensen in einem vollen Kreis ausschleudert
		'''
}

RebelBlade: {
	DisplayName: Rebellionsschwert
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Bei echtem Nahkampf erzeugt es eine Vielzahl von Dämonenlichtkugeln um das Ziel
		Rechtsklick steuert den Schwertflug und führt einen Schnitt zum Cursor hin aus
		'''
}

MG42: {
	DisplayName: MG42
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Dauerfeuer überhitzt das Maschinengewehr
		Verwandelt Gewehrkugeln in Panzerungsdurchschlagsgeschosse
		'''
}

DragonsWord: {
	DisplayName: Drachensprache
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Schieße Drachen Tränen ab, die nach einer Weile der Drehung den Feind verfolgen.
		Rechtsklick, um Drachenbefehl Magie am Cursor auszuführen und den Feinden im Bereich den Todesbefehl zu erteilen.
		"Ein Drache kann nur einmal im Leben weinen."
		'''
}

SnowQuayMK2: {
	DisplayName: Snow Quay MK2
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Verwendet Schneebälle als Munition
		Eine militärische Umrüstung mit Kryo-Kompressor, der Schneebälle in hochgeschwindige Eiszapfen presst
		Linksklick feuert Drei-Schuss-Bursts; die letzte Kugel ist ein schwerer Zapfen, der beim Treffer in Kristalle zerspringt
		Rechtsklick entlädt einen Überdruck-Streuschuss kurzreichweitiger Eiszapfen
		„Die Schneequay der zweiten Generation braucht kein Vorheizen mehr“
		'''
}

JusticeUnveiled: {
	DisplayName: Offenbarung der Gerechtigkeit
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Fernkampftreffer sammeln „Gerechtigkeitsschlag“-Ladungen, maximal fünf
		[UP] drücken verbraucht eine Ladung und ruft einen gewaltigen heiligen Einschlag herab
		„Wenn Gerechtigkeit gerufen wird, folge ich – wohin auch immer sie führt“
		'''
}

WeaverGrievances: {
	DisplayName: Gewebe der Groll
	// RitualInteractHint: "[Right-click] Pull the blade free"
	// RitualInventoryFullHint: Your inventory is full. Make room before pulling the blade.
	Tooltip:
		'''
		Entfesselt zahlreiche Klauen der Grollgeister mit Spezialeffekten in Unterwelt und Kerker
		Rechtsklick rollt zum Cursor; Treffer während des Rollens gewähren Unverwundbarkeitsframes
		Kurzer Rollen als perfektes Ausweichen
		[legend]
		'''
	Legend:
		'''
		Seit jeher erstreckt sich der Kerker über das Land, seine Wände bedeckt mit Inschriften und Schreien alter Krieger. Jeder Stein tränkt stillstehenden Groll, wie Ketten der Toten, gefesselt an die endlose Nacht am Ende der Zeit.
		Diese Krieger waren einst die Klingen ihres Königs und zogen in alle Welt. Ihr Schwertlicht erhellte Schlachtfelder, ihr Blut errichtete Terras Türme. Ihre Treue war sternenhaft, ihr Glaube eisern.
		Doch als die Kriege endeten und die Banner des Dämonfürsten vor dem König zerrissen wurden, befahl der König in dem Moment des Friedens, sie in die dunkle Tiefe zu werfen.
		„Wir haben nicht verraten, doch der König hat uns verraten!“ Die Klage der Verdammten riss die Stille der Ewigkeit.
		Ihre edlen Geister kehrten nicht zur Erde zurück, sondern wurden Zeugen der ewigen Nacht in der Leere.
		Das unaufhörliche Wehklagen wurde zum stillen Vorwurf bis ans Ende der Sterne.
		Man sagt, der König fürchtete ihre Treue und ihren Zorn und folgte den Flüstern der Dämonfürsten-Sorcerer, sie zur Schatten der Geschichte zu machen.
		Doch der Groll wie eine Flut verzerrte die Realität des Kerkers. Ihr Leid erschütterte die Schranken der Zeit und webte eine Racheprophezeiung:
		„Der Dämonfürst wird zurückkehren, die Leere wird zerreißen, unsere Flammen werden alles verzehren!“
		Wenn Terra erneut dem Untergang nahe, werden diese Untoten aus der Finsternis erstehen. Ihre Klingen kämpfen nicht mehr für Terras Ruhm, sondern zum Urteil des Verräters.
		Ruhm und Treue sind längst vernichtet – nur die Flammen der Rache bleiben.
		'''
}

Starship: {
